  "title": "A cut separates a path; a cycle of rings needs two",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "On rings of s >= 4 cells with rule 60 or 102 and contacts two apart, contacts reading their partners (the neighbour that reads them back) and the stated neighbours: (1) in RECUR-PROOF-20261108-001B's paths, the last contact needs R_0 at the lags with C(T - (n - 1), e) odd, and after freezing any one contact it needs R_0 at no lag while every other contact still binds both its rings at every lag, a native separation across time; (2) in four rings closed into a cycle with two routes from R_0 to R_2, every contact persists, R_2's contacts need R_0 exactly when C(T - 2, k1 - 1) or C(T - 2, k3 - 1) is odd, one cut removes exactly its route's lags, and a cut on each route separates R_0 from R_2.",
